                <head>124. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On September 28 Secretary of Defense McNamara, who had just returned to
                    Washington from a 2-day inspection trip in West Germany, stated at a press
                    conference that the Berlin situation, in military terms, was the most severe
                    since actual combat operations during the Korean war. McNamara then stated that
                    Khrushchev’s insistence on a peace treaty with East Germany and the consequent
                    restriction of U.S. rights in Berlin made the situation critical. To be sure
                    that there were no illusions in the Soviet Union, McNamara noted the United
                    States would “utilize whatever weapons are needed to preserve our vital
                    interests. Quite clearly, we consider access to Berlin a vital interest.” For
                    extracts from the press conference, see The New York Times, September 29,
                    1962.</p>
            </div>
